.TH hidppctl 1 "February 2026" "hidppctl"
.SH NAME
hidppctl \- Configure HID++ compatible peripherals.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
    \fBhidppctl\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]... \fI<subcommand>\fR
    \fBhidppctl\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]... \fIstatus\fR
    \fBhidppctl\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]... \fIdivert [buttons]...\fR
    \fBhidppctl\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]... \fIkeymap\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBhidppctl\fR enables configuration of peripherals that use the proprietary HID++ protocol.

.SS Remappable controls
.PP
\fBhidppctl\fR supports the HID++ remappable buttons feature.
To remap a control to another one, you can use the \fIremap\fR subcommand:

.nf
    hidppctl remap <control-id-or-name> <remap-id-or-name>
    hidppctl remap Page_Up Mute_Microphone
    hidppctl remap 0x104 0x105
.fi
.PP
To view a list of all available controls, run:

.nf
    hidppctl (your device) keymap
.fi

.PP
You can also divert controls to be handled by your application using the \fIdivert\fR subcommand:

.nf
    hidppctl divert [button-ids-or-names]...
    hidppctl divert Page_Up 0x104
.fi

.PP
Diverting controls will print their associated events to the standard output, for example:

.nf
    0x0104 Emoji_Smiley_Heart_Eyes press
    0x0104 Emoji_Smiley_Heart_Eyes release
    0x0104 Emoji_Smiley_Heart_Eyes press
    0x011A Page_Up press
    0x0104 Emoji_Smiley_Heart_Eyes release
    0x011A Page_Up release
.fi

.PP
\fBhidppctl\fR also supports simulating input so that you can turn otherwise
unresponsive controls into a shortcut with modifiers. This is done by adding
a \fB=\fR character and a keybind specification after it.

.nf
    hidppctl divert <control-id-or-name>=<key>[+modifiers...]
    hidppctl divert Emoji_Smiley_Heart_Eyes=Home
    hidppctl divert 0x11A=PageUp+LShift+LCtrl
.fi

.PP
Keybind can also use a platform-specific numeric code.
To view a list of all available keycode names, run:

.nf
    hidppctl list-keycodes
.fi

.SS Aliases
.PP
The options which pick a device (\fB\-\-receiver\fR, \fB\-\-socket\fR, \fB\-\-device\fR,
\fB\-\-interface\fR, \fB\-\-swid\fR and \fB\-\-timeout\fR) can be saved under a name:

.nf
    hidppctl \-r 046d:c548 \-d 1 alias set mouse
    hidppctl \-\-alias mouse dpi
    hidppctl alias [list|show <name>|remove <name>]
.fi
.PP
Options given next to \fB\-\-alias\fR override the saved ones. Aliases live in a
text file, one per line (\fIname key=value...\fR), in the user's configuration
directory; \fB\-\-alias\-file\fR selects another file.

.PP
\fBhidppctl search\fR looks for connected devices and, in an interactive terminal,
offers to save each one as an alias and to collect its cache.

.SS Other subcommands
.PP
Every subcommand shows the current state of its feature when it is run without
arguments. Arguments change the state. Run \fBhidppctl <subcommand> \-\-help\fR
for the exact syntax.

.TP
.B status
Lists receivers, the devices of a receiver (\fB-r\fR) or details of a device (\fB-d\fR).
.TP
.B battery, mode, nickname, host, platform
Battery state, performance mode, device nickname, multi-host management
(\fIlist\fR, \fIswitch\fR, \fIname\fR, \fImove\fR, \fIdelete\fR, \fIos\fR,
\fIcookie\fR) and the platform of a host.
.TP
.B action, disable-keys, fn-inversion
Persistent control actions (\fIset\fR, \fIreset\fR), disabled keys and the Fn key inversion.
Use \fB\-\-host\fR to select the host.
.TP
.B dpi, pointer, report-rate, scroll, smartshift, wheel, thumbwheel, crown
Pointing device configuration. Use \fB\-\-sensor\fR to select a motion sensor.
.TP
.B touchpad, touch-mouse
Raw touch reporting modes.
.TP
.B backlight, illumination, brightness, led, perkey
Lighting. Use \fB\-\-zone\fR to select a LED zone.
.TP
.B sidetone, eq, solar
Audio device and solar keyboard features.
.TP
.B poll
Prints incoming events, optionally only the given event types
(see \fBlist-events\fR).

.PP
Settings with several values use \fIname=value\fR pairs:

.nf
    hidppctl (your device) smartshift set mode=ratchet torque=60
    hidppctl (your device) led set fixed color=FF8800
    hidppctl (your device) host switch 1
.fi

.PP
Use \fB\-\-\fR before arguments which start with a minus sign:

.nf
    hidppctl (your device) eq set stored \-\- \-3 0 2 4 6
.fi

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-? ", " \-\-help
Shows help information and exits.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-receiver =\fIPATH-OR-ID\fR
Specifies which HID receiver to use. The argument can be a path to a raw device (\fI/dev/hidraw4\fR for example),
or a vendor and product id pair separated by \fB:\fR character (\fI046d:c548\fR for example).
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-device =\fIINDEX\fR
Specifies which device to pick from the receiver.
.TP
.BR \-\-host =\fIINDEX|current\fR
Selects the host for host-specific subcommands (\fIaction\fR, \fIfn-inversion\fR, \fIplatform\fR).
.TP
.BR \-\-sensor =\fIINDEX\fR
Selects the motion sensor used by \fIdpi\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-zone =\fIINDEX\fR
Selects the LED zone used by \fIled\fR (255 selects all zones).
.TP
.BR \-\-socket =\fIPATH|PORT|HOST:PORT\fR
Uses a receiver which is reachable through a Unix domain socket or a TCP
connection. A port alone connects to \fIlocalhost\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-log\-path =\fIFILE\fR
Logs the packets exchanged with the device to \fIFILE\fR. Logging is off by
default; \fB\-\-verbose\fR logs to a file in the runtime directory instead.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-log
Never log, even if \fB\-\-verbose\fR is given.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Shows the program version and exits.
.TP
.BR \-\-timeout =\fIMS\fR
Sets the timeout of IO operations in milliseconds.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful program execution.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred during processing or the arguments were invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hidppctl (1),
.BR libhidpp (3).
